  checkpoint {
    session_summary: "This is an incremental update for the \"Lalameow Rider\" project.\n\n### 1. Outstanding User Requests\n*   **Full Game Implementation (IMPLEMENTATION):** The core gameplay loop (delivery, GPS, fuel, phone, controls, etc.) remains to be built.\n*   **3D World/City Creation (NOT STARTED):** Build the living miniature city with NPCs and environmental props.\n*   **Camera System (NOT STARTED):** Implement the third-person 3D camera system with interpolation.\n*   **UI/HUD (NOT STARTED):** Create the persistent HUD, interactive map, and smartphone UI.\n*   **Progression & Customization (NOT STARTED):** Build out the systems for upgrading scooters and unlocking cosmetics.\n*   **Newspaper Delivery System (NOT STARTED):** Implement the optional newspaper delivery activity.\n*   **Settings & Tutorial (NOT STARTED):** Create the onboarding flow and settings menu.\n\n### 2. User Knowledge\n*   **Brand Identity:** Game name is \"Lalameow Rider\".\n*   **Visual Language:** \"Cute 3D cat delivery rider\", \"miniature city\", \"premium small-scale game\", \"not AI looking\".\n*   **Color Palette:** Warm honey yellow, dusty rose, and teal.\n*   **Architecture:** Clean modular architecture, separate state, avoid monolithic files.\n*   **Device:** Mobile-first, touchscreen controls.\n*   **Instructional Context:** The user explicitly requested a \"polished, adorable, highly interactive 3D mobile-first delivery-rider game\" with a \"playful personality\" and \"rapid prototyping quality associated with Claude Fable.\"\n\n### 3. Work Accomplished\n*   **Project Initialization:** Set up Android project structure, updated `settings.gradle.kts`, `strings.xml`, `AndroidManifest.xml`, and `build.gradle.kts` (application ID).\n*   **Branding:** Generated `img_app_icon` and `img_hero_banner`. Configured adaptive icons.\n*   **Theming:** Updated `Color.kt` and `Theme.kt` with the custom \"Lalameow Rider\" brand palette (Honey Yellow, Dusty Rose, Teal).\n*   **Audio Engine:** Created `GameSoundEngine.kt` for procedural audio synthesis (engine, horn, notifications, haptics). This avoids external asset dependencies for core sounds.\n*   **Data Models:** Created `CatCustomization.kt` to handle character and scooter configuration options.\n*   **Compilation:** Verified baseline compilation of the project.\n\n### 4. Model Knowledge\n*   **Architecture:** Using Jetpack Compose with Material 3. The theme is configured in `Theme.kt` using custom brand colors defined in `Color.kt`.\n*   **Audio:** Procedural synthesis is implemented via `AudioTrack` (low-latency). The `GameSoundEngine` manages haptics and continuous engine loops.\n*   **State Management:** The project needs to implement MVVM (ViewModel + StateFlow) to handle game state (fuel, delivery status, position) as the next step.\n*   **Constraints:** Avoid generic AI aesthetics. Prioritize tactile, responsive UI.\n\n### 5. Files and Code\n*   **Edited Files:**\n    *   `/app/src/main/res/drawable/ic_launcher_foreground.xml`: Updated to use the custom generated app icon.\n    *   `/app/src/main/res/drawable/ic_launcher_background.xml`: Updated to a gradient background matching the brand palette.\n    *   `/app/build.gradle.kts`: Set unique `applicationId`.\n    *   `/metadata.json`: Updated name and description.\n    *   `/app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml`: Updated app name.\n    *   `/settings.gradle.kts`: Updated project name.\n    *   `/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`: Added `VIBRATE` permission.\n    *   `/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/theme/Color.kt`: Added brand palette.\n    *   `/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/theme/Theme.kt`: Updated color scheme to use brand colors.\n*   **Created Files:**\n    *   `/app/src/main/java/com/example/game/audio/GameSoundEngine.kt`: Procedural audio engine.\n    *   `/app/src/main/java/com/example/game/model/CatCustomization.kt`: Data models for customization.\n*   **Viewed Files (Relevant for next steps):**\n    *   `/app/src/main/java/com/example/MainActivity.kt`: The entry point for the UI.\n    *   `/app/src/test/java/com/example/GreetingScreenshotTest.kt`: Reference for testing.\n    *   `/gradle/libs.versions.toml`: Dependency management.\n    *   `/skills/system_skills/design_guidelines/SKILL.md`: Design principles for the UI.\n\n### 6. Current Work and Next Steps\n*   **Current Status:** The foundation (branding, audio engine, data models) is ready.\n*   **Next Steps:**\n    1.  **Implement Game State:** Create a `GameViewModel` to manage the game state (player position, fuel, delivery status).\n    2.  **Build UI:** Implement the main game screen (HUD, map, and touchscreen controls) using Jetpack Compose.\n    3.  **Integrate Audio:** Connect `GameSoundEngine` to the UI actions (e.g., horn button, delivery success).\n    4.  **3D Scene:** Begin integrating the 3D environment and camera system.\n\n*   **Relevant Skills:**\n    *   `/skills/system_skills/design_guidelines/SKILL.md`: Essential for maintaining the \"premium, adorable\" aesthetic in Compose.\n    *   `/skills/system_skills/image_generation/SKILL.md`: Useful for generating future in-game assets."
    included_step_index_end: 56
    conversation_log_uris: "file:///.aistudio/artifacts/brain/675fbede-1bc6-453c-9800-b093ac9af82c/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl"
    user_requests: "User prompt: Build a polished, adorable, highly interactive 3D mobile-first delivery-rider game inspired by the general concept of real-world delivery platforms, but with a completely original identity, visual language, characters, interface, gameplay, naming, and mechanics. Do not copy Lalamove, Grab, or any other existing delivery company\'s branding, UI, assets, characters, logos, terminology, or copyrighted design. The central character is a cute 3D cat delivery rider who travels around a lively miniature city completing deliveries. Give the entire experience the feeling of a premium small-scale game made with Unreal Engine, with the playful personality and rapid prototyping quality associated with Claude Fable. The game should feel tactile, smooth, charming, responsive, and surprisingly deep rather than like a static website pretending to be a game.\n\nCreate an original witty brand name for the game and app, something short, memorable, playful, and cat-related without sounding like a direct parody of existing delivery companies. The player begins by creating and naming their own cat rider. Let the player choose or customize the cat\'s name, appearance, fur color, helmet, delivery jacket, backpack, scooter or small motorcycle, and other cosmetic details. The cat should have expressive animations, subtle personality, ear movements, blinking, tail movement, reactions to traffic, weather, customers, notifications, fuel, successful deliveries, mistakes, and funny situations. Make the rider feel alive even when idle.\n\nThe primary gameplay loop should be: receive a delivery request, inspect the order, accept or decline it, travel to the pickup location using an interactive GPS/navigation system, arrive at the pickup point, collect the package, navigate to the customer\'s destination, deliver it, receive payment/reputation/rewards, and continue accepting new jobs. Include multiple delivery categories such as food, parcels, groceries, flowers, documents, newspapers, books, and humorous fictional items. Occasionally present the cat with small choices, such as whether to accept a newspaper delivery, take a shortcut, stop for food, refuel, explore a side street, or wait for another higher-paying delivery.\n\nGive the cat a functional in-game smartphone. The phone should occasionally vibrate or display notifications for new delivery requests, customer messages, navigation updates, payment confirmations, weather warnings, fuel warnings, promotional offers, and silly cat-related messages. Create a beautiful compact phone UI that can be opened and closed during gameplay. Notifications should feel contextual rather than random. For example, the cat might be riding through the city when the phone displays: \"New delivery nearby!\" or \"Customer is waiting at the pickup point.\" Include a notification history so the player can review previous messages.\n\nCreate a complete touchscreen control system designed primarily for mobile devices. The player should be able to control the cat and vehicle using large, responsive virtual controls: left/right steering, acceleration, brake/reverse, horn, handbrake or drift, interaction/action button, camera control, map button, phone button, and delivery confirmation button. Support touch-and-drag steering where appropriate. Make every controller feel smooth and responsive, with subtle animation and haptic-style visual feedback. Controls should intelligently disappear or become less intrusive when they are not needed. Include a settings menu allowing players to adjust control sensitivity, controller size, camera sensitivity, vibration/haptic feedback, graphics quality, sound, music, and accessibility options.\n\nBuild a full third-person 3D camera system. Include a driving camera behind the cat, a slightly elevated navigation camera, and an optional free-look camera. Add smooth camera interpolation rather than abrupt camera movement. The camera should react naturally to acceleration, braking, turning, bumps, jumps, collisions, and sudden stops. Include a cinematic camera mode for successful deliveries and special moments.\n\nCreate a persistent HUD. The HUD should contain a small live GPS map in one corner showing the player\'s current location, roads, pickup location, destination, route, nearby points of interest, and directional indicators. Use an original visual style rather than copying Google Maps, Grab, Lalamove, or other navigation interfaces. The GPS route should dynamically update if the player goes off-route. Show distance remaining, estimated travel time, current street or neighborhood, and directional prompts. Allow the player to expand the map into a larger interactive map screen. Include map markers for gas stations, restaurants, shops, repair stations, delivery locations, and optional exploration areas.\n\nAdd a proper vehicle fuel system. The cat\'s scooter or motorcycle should have a visible fuel gauge on the HUD. Fuel decreases gradually while riding. Display low-fuel warnings through both the HUD and the cat\'s phone. Place gas stations throughout the city where the player can stop and refuel. Make refueling interactive: pull into the gas station, stop near the pump, press the interaction button, and watch the fuel meter rise. Give the player the option to purchase different amounts of fuel rather than forcing a full tank. Running out of fuel should not simply end the game. Instead, trigger a funny recovery situation where the cat has to push the scooter, call roadside assistance, or pay a small service fee.\n\nCreate a living miniature city designed specifically for the game. Include residential streets, apartment buildings, restaurants, convenience stores, offices, schools, parks, markets, gas stations, warehouses, pickup hubs, pedestrian areas, traffic lights, crosswalks, alleys, shortcuts, bridges, and scenic areas. Populate the environment with pedestrians, cars, bicycles, other delivery riders, shopkeepers, customers, and other anthropomorphic animals. Make the world feel playful and slightly exaggerated rather than photorealistic. Use beautiful 3D lighting, soft shadows, volumetric atmosphere, reflections, ambient animation, moving clouds, subtle wind, and detailed environmental props.\n\nImplement a dynamic day-and-night cycle and weather system. Allow sunny weather, cloudy weather, light rain, heavy rain, sunset, nighttime, and other atmospheric conditions. Weather should affect gameplay subtly. Rain can reduce visibility and make roads slightly slippery. Nighttime can increase delivery rewards. Weather notifications can arrive through the cat\'s phone. Make the environment visually transform depending on time and weather.\n\nCreate NPC interactions. Customers should have distinct personalities and short contextual dialogue. When the cat arrives at the pickup or delivery point, allow the player to interact with the character using a simple touchscreen interaction system. Customers can compliment the cat, complain about being late, thank the rider, give tips, or make humorous comments. Keep dialogue short and charming. Give important NPCs memorable visual personalities.\n\nCreate a delivery-request screen that appears when a new job arrives. Show pickup location, destination, estimated distance, estimated time, package type, reward, fuel cost estimate, difficulty, and any special conditions. Give the player ACCEPT and DECLINE choices. Some deliveries should be urgent, some long-distance, some high-paying, some easy, and some funny. Introduce occasional multi-stop deliveries and chained delivery routes once the player progresses.\n\nCreate a progression system. Completing deliveries earns coins, experience, reputation, and unlockable cosmetic items. The player can upgrade the cat\'s scooter or motorcycle, including fuel capacity, acceleration, handling, braking, storage capacity, durability, and navigation equipment. Keep upgrades balanced and make the game playable without requiring purchases. Add cosmetic customization for helmets, jackets, backpacks, scooters, stickers, horns, cat accessories, and riding animations.\n\nAdd a reputation system based on successful deliveries, punctuality, customer satisfaction, safe driving, and completed jobs. Higher reputation unlocks new neighborhoods, delivery types, customers, vehicles, accessories, and special missions. Include daily challenges such as \"Complete five deliveries,\" \"Visit three neighborhoods,\" \"Finish a delivery without running out of fuel,\" or \"Deliver three newspapers.\"\n\nInclude a newspaper delivery system as a recurring optional activity. Newspapers can arrive as a special category of delivery, with multiple newspapers loaded into the cat\'s delivery bag. The player can choose whether to accept these jobs. Create charming animations where the cat picks up newspapers and delivers them to houses, shops, or offices. Some newspaper deliveries can contain humorous fictional headlines that add personality to the game world.\n\nMake navigation intelligent. The GPS should understand the road network, calculate routes, recalculate after wrong turns, identify the closest gas station when fuel is low, and provide simple visual navigation cues. Avoid overwhelming the player with excessive text. Use arrows, icons, distance counters, subtle sounds, and environmental landmarks.\n\nCreate a complete pause/settings screen and a lightweight onboarding tutorial. The first few minutes should teach movement, steering, acceleration, braking, accepting deliveries, GPS navigation, pickup interaction, delivery interaction, fuel, and the smartphone. Make the tutorial feel like gameplay rather than a long instructional screen.\n\nAdd sound design throughout the experience: scooter engine sounds, tires, brakes, horns, rain, birds, city ambience, phone vibrations, notification sounds, pickup sounds, delivery-complete sounds, coin sounds, fuel-pump sounds, UI taps, cat meows, purrs, surprised reactions, and short musical cues. Give the cat subtle vocal reactions without making constant noises.\n\nMake the visual design premium, adorable, modern, and highly polished. Think miniature 3D animated world, expressive collectible-toy characters, cinematic lighting, clean mobile UI, tactile buttons, rounded interfaces, subtle glass-like panels, smooth transitions, and sophisticated motion design. Avoid generic AI-generated dashboard aesthetics. Every interface element should serve gameplay.\n\nOptimize the entire experience for touchscreens first while ensuring the architecture can later support keyboard/controller input. Make buttons large enough for comfortable mobile interaction. Include responsive layouts for phone and tablet screens. Prioritize performance with optimized 3D assets, level-of-detail systems, efficient particle effects, lazy loading, compressed textures, object pooling, and sensible scene management.\n\nMost importantly, make the application actually feel playable. Do not create a collection of disconnected screens or a fake interactive prototype. Implement the core gameplay state, player movement, vehicle movement, delivery jobs, pickup and destination coordinates, GPS navigation, fuel consumption, gas stations, smartphone notifications, touchscreen controls, NPC interactions, rewards, progression, HUD, map, camera system, day/night cycle, weather, and save/load state as functional systems wherever the environment supports them. If a requested feature cannot be implemented fully in the current environment, create a realistic working approximation and clearly structure the code so the feature can later be connected to a proper 3D/game engine.\n\nUse clean modular architecture. Separate player state, vehicle state, delivery state, navigation state, fuel state, economy, notifications, NPC interactions, UI/HUD, world systems, audio, settings, and persistence. Avoid one enormous component or monolithic file. Keep data structures extensible so new delivery types, neighborhoods, vehicles, NPCs, missions, and cosmetic items can be added easily.\n\nThe final experience should feel like \"a tiny cat has a real job and a whole city to explore.\" The player should care about the cat, not just the delivery mechanics. Give the world humor, warmth, tiny surprises, and moments that make players smile. The cat might stop beside a puddle and shake its paws after getting wet, look at its phone while waiting for a customer, become visibly tired after a long route, celebrate after receiving a large tip, or stare dramatically at the fuel gauge when the tank is nearly empty. These small details should make the game memorable.\n\nBefore implementation, establish the game\'s original brand identity, color system, typography, icon language, character style, world style, interaction principles, and gameplay architecture. Then build the experience around those foundations. Prioritize a polished playable vertical slice first: customizable cat + scooter + small city district + touchscreen controls + delivery request + pickup + GPS route + destination + delivery completion + reward + phone notifications + fuel + gas station + HUD map. Once that loop works smoothly, expand the world and feature set.\n\nThe final result should feel less like a delivery-service website and more like a charming, polished, miniature 3D adventure game about a cat trying to make its way through the city, one delivery at a time.\n\nName for this game is Lalameow Rider. Generate custom branding, icon, etc. for the app to capture that whimsical aesthetic. Create a color palette featuring soft pastels and a vibrant, playful logo. The primary brand colors will include warm honey yellow, dusty rose, and teal. Create the assets now. Very cute game, but not AI looking. \n\n\nHere is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/app/.gitignore\n/app/build.gradle.kts\n/app/proguard-rules.pro\n/app/src/androidTest/java/com/example/ExampleInstrumentedTest.kt\n/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml\n/app/src/main/java/com/example/MainActivity.kt\n/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/theme/Color.kt\n/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/theme/Theme.kt\n/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/theme/Type.kt\n/app/src/main/res/drawable/ic_launcher_background.xml\n/app/src/main/res/drawable/ic_launcher_foreground.xml\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher_round.xml\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-hdpi/ic_launcher.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-hdpi/ic_launcher_round.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-mdpi/ic_launcher.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-mdpi/ic_launcher_round.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-xhdpi/ic_launcher.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-xhdpi/ic_launcher_round.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-xxhdpi/ic_launcher.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-xxhdpi/ic_launcher_round.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-xxxhdpi/ic_launcher.webp\n/app/src/main/res/mipmap-xxxhdpi/ic_launcher_round.webp\n/app/src/main/res/values/colors.xml\n/app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml\n/app/src/main/res/values/themes.xml\n/app/src/main/res/xml/backup_rules.xml\n/app/src/main/res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml\n/app/src/test/java/com/example/ExampleRobolectricTest.kt\n/app/src/test/java/com/example/ExampleUnitTest.kt\n/app/src/test/java/com/example/GreetingScreenshotTest.kt\n/app/src/test/screenshots/greeting.png\n/build.gradle.kts\n/gradle.properties\n/gradle/libs.versions.toml\n/gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties\n/metadata.json\n/public/assets/aistudio/.gitignore\n/settings.gradle.kts\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# This is a placeholder key.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\n# IMPORTANT: Uncomment the line below if your app uses the Gemini API.\n# If left commented out, the key will NOT be packaged in the APK.\n# GEMINI_API_KEY=MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\n\n```\n\n--- /app/build.gradle.kts ---\n```\nimport com.google.gms.googleservices.GoogleServicesPlugin.MissingGoogleServicesStrategy\n\nplugins {\n  alias(libs.plugins.android.application)\n  alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose)\n  alias(libs.plugins.google.devtools.ksp)\n  alias(libs.plugins.roborazzi)\n  alias(libs.plugins.secrets)\n  alias(libs.plugins.google.services)\n}\n\nandroid {\n  namespace = \"com.example\"\n  compileSdk { version = release(36) { minorApiLevel = 1 } }\n\n  defaultConfig {\n    applicationId = \"com.example\"\n    minSdk = 24\n    targetSdk = 36\n    versionCode = 1\n    versionName = \"1.0\"\n\n    testInstrumentationRunner = \"androidx.test.runner.AndroidJUnitRunner\"\n  }\n\n  signingConfigs {\n    create(\"release\") {\n      val keystorePath = System.getenv(\"KEYSTORE_PATH\") ?: \"${rootDir}/my-upload-key.jks\"\n      storeFile = file(keystorePath)\n      storePassword = System.getenv(\"STORE_PASSWORD\")\n      keyAlias = \"upload\"\n      keyPassword = System.getenv(\"KEY_PASSWORD\")\n    }\n    create(\"debugConfig\") {\n      storeFile = file(\"${rootDir}/debug.keystore\")\n      storePassword = \"android\"\n      keyAlias = \"androiddebugkey\"\n      keyPassword = \"android\"\n    }\n  }\n\n  buildTypes {\n    release {\n      isCrunchPngs = false\n      isMinifyEnabled = false\n      proguardFiles(getDefaultProguardFile(\"proguard-android-optimize.txt\"), \"proguard-rules.pro\")\n      signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"release\")\n    }\n    debug { signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"debugConfig\") }\n  }\n  compileOptions {\n    sourceCompatibility = JavaVersion.VERSION_11\n    targetCompatibility = JavaVersion.VERSION_11\n  }\n  buildFeatures {\n    compose = true\n    buildConfig = true\n  }\n  testOptions { unitTests { isIncludeAndroidResources = true } }\n  dependenciesInfo {\n    includeInApk = false\n    includeInBundle = true\n  }\n}\n\n// Configure the Secrets Gradle Plugin to use .env and .env.example files\n// to match the convention used in Web projects.\nsecrets {\n  propertiesFileName = \".env\"\n  defaultPropertiesFileName = \".env.example\"\n  ignoreList.add(\"FIREBASE_APPCHECK_DEBUG_TOKEN\")\n}\n\ngoogleServices { missingGoogleServicesStrategy = MissingGoogleServicesStrategy.WARN }\n\n// Some unused dependencies are commented out below instead of being removed.\n// This makes it easy to add them back in the future if needed.\ndependencies {\n  implementation(platform(libs.androidx.compose.bom))\n  implementation(platform(libs.firebase.bom))\n  // implementation(libs.accompanist.permissions)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.activity.compose)\n  // implementation(libs.androidx.camera.camera2)\n  // implementation(libs.androidx.camera.core)\n  // implementation(libs.androidx.camera.lifecycle)\n  // implementation(libs.androidx.camera.view)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.compose.material.icons.core)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.compose.material.icons.extended)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.compose.material3)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui.graphics)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui.tooling.preview)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.core.ktx)\n  // implementation(libs.androidx.datastore.preferences)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.lifecycle.runtime.compose)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.lifecycle.runtime.ktx)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.lifecycle.viewmodel.compose)\n  // implementation(libs.androidx.navigation.compose)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.room.ktx)\n  implementation(libs.androidx.room.runtime)\n  // implementation(libs.coil.compose)\n  implementation(libs.converter.moshi)\n  implementation(libs.firebase.ai)\n  // Uncomment to use Firestore:\n  // implementation(libs.firebase.firestore)\n\n  // Uncomment ALL FOUR of the following dependencies together to use Firebase Auth and Google\n  // Sign-In via Credential Manager:\n  // implementation(libs.firebase.auth)\n  // implementation(libs.androidx.credentials)\n  // implementation(libs.androidx.credentials.play.services)\n  // implementation(libs.googleid)\n  implementation(libs.firebase.appcheck.recaptcha)\n  implementation(libs.firebase.appcheck.debug)\n  implementation(libs.kotlinx.coroutines.android)\n  implementation(libs.kotlinx.coroutines.core)\n  implementation(libs.logging.interceptor)\n  implementation(libs.moshi.kotlin)\n  implementation(libs.okhttp)\n  // implementation(libs.play.services.location)\n  implementation(libs.retrofit)\n  testImplementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4)\n  testImplementation(libs.androidx.core)\n  testImplementation(libs.androidx.junit)\n  testImplementation(libs.junit)\n  testImplementation(libs.kotlinx.coroutines.test)\n  testImplementation(libs.robolectric)\n  testImplementation(libs.roborazzi)\n  testImplementation(libs.roborazzi.compose)\n  testImplementation(libs.roborazzi.junit.rule)\n  androidTestImplementation(platform(libs.androidx.compose.bom))\n  androidTestImplementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4)\n  androidTestImplementation(libs.androidx.espresso.core)\n  androidTestImplementation(libs.androidx.junit)\n  androidTestImplementation(libs.androidx.runner)\n  debugImplementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui.test.manifest)\n  debugImplementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui.tooling)\n  \"ksp\"(libs.androidx.room.compiler)\n  \"ksp\"(libs.moshi.kotlin.codegen)\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /build.gradle.kts ---\n```\n// Top-level build file where you can add configuration options common to all sub-projects/modules.\nplugins {\n  alias(libs.plugins.android.application) apply false\n  alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose) apply false\n  alias(libs.plugins.google.devtools.ksp) apply false\n  alias(libs.plugins.roborazzi) apply false\n  alias(libs.plugins.secrets) apply false\n  alias(libs.plugins.google.services) apply false\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```"
    user_requests: "Build a polished, adorable, highly interactive 3D mobile-first delivery-rider game inspired by the general concept of real-world delivery platforms, but with a completely original identity, visual language, characters, interface, gameplay, naming, and mechanics. Do not copy Lalamove, Grab, or any other existing delivery company\'s branding, UI, assets, characters, logos, terminology, or copyrighted design. The central character is a cute 3D cat delivery rider who travels around a lively miniature city completing deliveries. Give the entire experience the feeling of a premium small-scale game made with Unreal Engine, with the playful personality and rapid prototyping quality associated with Claude Fable. The game should feel tactile, smooth, charming, responsive, and surprisingly deep rather than like a static website pretending to be a game.\n\nCreate an original witty brand name for the game and app, something short, memorable, playful, and cat-related without sounding like a direct parody of existing delivery companies. The player begins by creating and naming their own cat rider. Let the player choose or customize the cat\'s name, appearance, fur color, helmet, delivery jacket, backpack, scooter or small motorcycle, and other cosmetic details. The cat should have expressive animations, subtle personality, ear movements, blinking, tail movement, reactions to traffic, weather, customers, notifications, fuel, successful deliveries, mistakes, and funny situations. Make the rider feel alive even when idle.\n\nThe primary gameplay loop should be: receive a delivery request, inspect the order, accept or decline it, travel to the pickup location using an interactive GPS/navigation system, arrive at the pickup point, collect the package, navigate to the customer\'s destination, deliver it, receive payment/reputation/rewards, and continue accepting new jobs. Include multiple delivery categories such as food, parcels, groceries, flowers, documents, newspapers, books, and humorous fictional items. Occasionally present the cat with small choices, such as whether to accept a newspaper delivery, take a shortcut, stop for food, refuel, explore a side street, or wait for another higher-paying delivery.\n\nGive the cat a functional in-game smartphone. The phone should occasionally vibrate or display notifications for new delivery requests, customer messages, navigation updates, payment confirmations, weather warnings, fuel warnings, promotional offers, and silly cat-related messages. Create a beautiful compact phone UI that can be opened and closed during gameplay. Notifications should feel contextual rather than random. For example, the cat might be riding through the city when the phone displays: \"New delivery nearby!\" or \"Customer is waiting at the pickup point.\" Include a notification history so the player can review previous messages.\n\nCreate a complete touchscreen control system designed primarily for mobile devices. The player should be able to control the cat and vehicle using large, responsive virtual controls: left/right steering, acceleration, brake/reverse, horn, handbrake or drift, interaction/action button, camera control, map button, phone button, and delivery confirmation button. Support touch-and-drag steering where appropriate. Make every controller feel smooth and responsive, with subtle animation and haptic-style visual feedback. Controls should intelligently disappear or become less intrusive when they are not needed. Include a settings menu allowing players to adjust control sensitivity, controller size, camera sensitivity, vibration/haptic feedback, graphics quality, sound, music, and accessibility options.\n\nBuild a full third-person 3D camera system. Include a driving camera behind the cat, a slightly elevated navigation camera, and an optional free-look camera. Add smooth camera interpolation rather than abrupt camera movement. The camera should react naturally to acceleration, braking, turning, bumps, jumps, collisions, and sudden stops. Include a cinematic camera mode for successful deliveries and special moments.\n\nCreate a persistent HUD. The HUD should contain a small live GPS map in one corner showing the player\'s current location, roads, pickup location, destination, route, nearby points of interest, and directional indicators. Use an original visual style rather than copying Google Maps, Grab, Lalamove, or other navigation interfaces. The GPS route should dynamically update if the player goes off-route. Show distance remaining, estimated travel time, current street or neighborhood, and directional prompts. Allow the player to expand the map into a larger interactive map screen. Include map markers for gas stations, restaurants, shops, repair stations, delivery locations, and optional exploration areas.\n\nAdd a proper vehicle fuel system. The cat\'s scooter or motorcycle should have a visible fuel gauge on the HUD. Fuel decreases gradually while riding. Display low-fuel warnings through both the HUD and the cat\'s phone. Place gas stations throughout the city where the player can stop and refuel. Make refueling interactive: pull into the gas station, stop near the pump, press the interaction button, and watch the fuel meter rise. Give the player the option to purchase different amounts of fuel rather than forcing a full tank. Running out of fuel should not simply end the game. Instead, trigger a funny recovery situation where the cat has to push the scooter, call roadside assistance, or pay a small service fee.\n\nCreate a living miniature city designed specifically for the game. Include residential streets, apartment buildings, restaurants, convenience stores, offices, schools, parks, markets, gas stations, warehouses, pickup hubs, pedestrian areas, traffic lights, crosswalks, alleys, shortcuts, bridges, and scenic areas. Populate the environment with pedestrians, cars, bicycles, other delivery riders, shopkeepers, customers, and other anthropomorphic animals. Make the world feel playful and slightly exaggerated rather than photorealistic. Use beautiful 3D lighting, soft shadows, volumetric atmosphere, reflections, ambient animation, moving clouds, subtle wind, and detailed environmental props.\n\nImplement a dynamic day-and-night cycle and weather system. Allow sunny weather, cloudy weather, light rain, heavy rain, sunset, nighttime, and other atmospheric conditions. Weather should affect gameplay subtly. Rain can reduce visibility and make roads slightly slippery. Nighttime can increase delivery rewards. Weather notifications can arrive through the cat\'s phone. Make the environment visually transform depending on time and weather.\n\nCreate NPC interactions. Customers should have distinct personalities and short contextual dialogue. When the cat arrives at the pickup or delivery point, allow the player to interact with the character using a simple touchscreen interaction system. Customers can compliment the cat, complain about being late, thank the rider, give tips, or make humorous comments. Keep dialogue short and charming. Give important NPCs memorable visual personalities.\n\nCreate a delivery-request screen that appears when a new job arrives. Show pickup location, destination, estimated distance, estimated time, package type, reward, fuel cost estimate, difficulty, and any special conditions. Give the player ACCEPT and DECLINE choices. Some deliveries should be urgent, some long-distance, some high-paying, some easy, and some funny. Introduce occasional multi-stop deliveries and chained delivery routes once the player progresses.\n\nCreate a progression system. Completing deliveries earns coins, experience, reputation, and unlockable cosmetic items. The player can upgrade the cat\'s scooter or motorcycle, including fuel capacity, acceleration, handling, braking, storage capacity, durability, and navigation equipment. Keep upgrades balanced and make the game playable without requiring purchases. Add cosmetic customization for helmets, jackets, backpacks, scooters, stickers, horns, cat accessories, and riding animations.\n\nAdd a reputation system based on successful deliveries, punctuality, customer satisfaction, safe driving, and completed jobs. Higher reputation unlocks new neighborhoods, delivery types, customers, vehicles, accessories, and special missions. Include daily challenges such as \"Complete five deliveries,\" \"Visit three neighborhoods,\" \"Finish a delivery without running out of fuel,\" or \"Deliver three newspapers.\"\n\nInclude a newspaper delivery system as a recurring optional activity. Newspapers can arrive as a special category of delivery, with multiple newspapers loaded into the cat\'s delivery bag. The player can choose whether to accept these jobs. Create charming animations where the cat picks up newspapers and delivers them to houses, shops, or offices. Some newspaper deliveries can contain humorous fictional headlines that add personality to the game world.\n\nMake navigation intelligent. The GPS should understand the road network, calculate routes, recalculate after wrong turns, identify the closest gas station when fuel is low, and provide simple visual navigation cues. Avoid overwhelming the player with excessive text. Use arrows, icons, distance counters, subtle sounds, and environmental landmarks.\n\nCreate a complete pause/settings screen and a lightweight onboarding tutorial. The first few minutes should teach movement, steering, acceleration, braking, accepting deliveries, GPS navigation, pickup interaction, delivery interaction, fuel, and the smartphone. Make the tutorial feel like gameplay rather than a long instructional screen.\n\nAdd sound design throughout the experience: scooter engine sounds, tires, brakes, horns, rain, birds, city ambience, phone vibrations, notification sounds, pickup sounds, delivery-complete sounds, coin sounds, fuel-pump sounds, UI taps, cat meows, purrs, surprised reactions, and short musical cues. Give the cat subtle vocal reactions without making constant noises.\n\nMake the visual design premium, adorable, modern, and highly polished. Think miniature 3D animated world, expressive collectible-toy characters, cinematic lighting, clean mobile UI, tactile buttons, rounded interfaces, subtle glass-like panels, smooth transitions, and sophisticated motion design. Avoid generic AI-generated dashboard aesthetics. Every interface element should serve gameplay.\n\nOptimize the entire experience for touchscreens first while ensuring the architecture can later support keyboard/controller input. Make buttons large enough for comfortable mobile interaction. Include responsive layouts for phone and tablet screens. Prioritize performance with optimized 3D assets, level-of-detail systems, efficient particle effects, lazy loading, compressed textures, object pooling, and sensible scene management.\n\nMost importantly, make the application actually feel playable. Do not create a collection of disconnected screens or a fake interactive prototype. Implement the core gameplay state, player movement, vehicle movement, delivery jobs, pickup and destination coordinates, GPS navigation, fuel consumption, gas stations, smartphone notifications, touchscreen controls, NPC interactions, rewards, progression, HUD, map, camera system, day/night cycle, weather, and save/load state as functional systems wherever the environment supports them. If a requested feature cannot be implemented fully in the current environment, create a realistic working approximation and clearly structure the code so the feature can later be connected to a proper 3D/game engine.\n\nUse clean modular architecture. Separate player state, vehicle state, delivery state, navigation state, fuel state, economy, notifications, NPC interactions, UI/HUD, world systems, audio, settings, and persistence. Avoid one enormous component or monolithic file. Keep data structures extensible so new delivery types, neighborhoods, vehicles, NPCs, missions, and cosmetic items can be added easily.\n\nThe final experience should feel like \"a tiny cat has a real job and a whole city to explore.\" The player should care about the cat, not just the delivery mechanics. Give the world humor, warmth, tiny surprises, and moments that make players smile. The cat might stop beside a puddle and shake its paws after getting wet, look at its phone while waiting for a customer, become visibly tired after a long route, celebrate after receiving a large tip, or stare dramatically at the fuel gauge when the tank is nearly empty. These small details should make the game memorable.\n\nBefore implementation, establish the game\'s original brand identity, color system, typography, icon language, character style, world style, interaction principles, and gameplay architecture. Then build the experience around those foundations. Prioritize a polished playable vertical slice first: customizable cat + scooter + small city district + touchscreen controls + delivery request + pickup + GPS route + destination + delivery completion + reward + phone notifications + fuel + gas station + HUD map. Once that loop works smoothly, expand the world and feature set.\n\nThe final result should feel less like a delivery-service website and more like a charming, polished, miniature 3D adventure game about a cat trying to make its way through the city, one delivery at a time.\n\nName for this game is Lalameow Rider. Generate custom branding, icon, etc. for the app to capture that whimsical aesthetic. Create a color palette featuring soft pastels and a vibrant, playful logo. The primary brand colors will include warm honey yellow, dusty rose, and teal. Create the assets now. Very cute game, but not AI looking. \n"
    total_subagent_count: 0
  }
}
