.\n\nCreate **vehicle maintenance**. Vehicles gradually accumulate wear. Add tire condition, engine condition, brake condition, and cosmetic damage. Let the player visit a repair shop. Repairs should be simple, fast, and fun rather than tedious.\n\nCreate multiple vehicles that unlock through progression: basic scooter, upgraded scooter, tiny motorcycle, electric scooter, compact delivery motorcycle, retro scooter, futuristic scooter, and other original fictional vehicles. Give each vehicle different acceleration, handling, storage, fuel efficiency, and top speed.\n\nCreate a **vehicle customization garage**. Let players customize colors, decals, wheels, mirrors, delivery boxes, headlights, horns, seat design, license plate style, and accessories. Include cat-themed cosmetic items.\n\nCreate **delivery backpacks** with different capacities. Larger bags allow more packages but may affect handling. Unlock new bags through progression.\n\nCreate **character progression**. Give the cat experience points, rider levels, reputation, achievements, and skill upgrades. Skills can include navigation, handling, fuel efficiency, package handling, customer service, delivery speed, and storage.\n\nCreate a **skill tree** with meaningful but balanced upgrades. Make progression visually satisfying.\n\nCreate a **daily mission system** with rotating objectives such as:\n\n\"Complete 5 deliveries.\"\n\"Earn 500 coins.\"\n\"Visit three districts.\"\n\"Refuel twice.\"\n\"Deliver 3 newspapers.\"\n\"Complete a delivery during rain.\"\n\"Finish a route without crashing.\"\n\"Receive three customer tips.\"\n\"Discover a hidden shortcut.\"\n\nCreate weekly challenges and milestone achievements.\n\nCreate **badges and trophies** for accomplishments such as:\n\nFirst Delivery\nFirst Tip\nPerfect Delivery\nNight Rider\nRain Rider\nFuel Saver\nNewspaper Cat\nSpeedy Paws\nCity Explorer\nFive-Star Cat\nNeighborhood Hero\nLong Haul\nPerfect Route\nNo GPS Challenge\nGas Station Regular\n\nCreate an **achievement gallery** where the player can view unlocked badges.\n\nCreate an **interactive smartphone** that the cat carries. The phone should have multiple apps:\n\nDelivery Jobs\nGPS\nMessages\nWallet\nRider Profile\nAchievements\nWeather\nNews\nSettings\nGarage\nNotifications\n\nThe phone should occasionally vibrate or display notifications while the player is riding. Notifications can include new delivery offers, customer messages, fuel warnings, weather alerts, nearby events, achievement unlocks, payment confirmations, tips, and fictional city news.\n\nCreate contextual phone notifications. For example:\n\n\"\360\237\220\276 New delivery nearby!\"\n\"Customer added a tip!\"\n\"Rain expected in 5 minutes.\"\n\"Fuel is getting low.\"\n\"Newspaper route available.\"\n\"Your reputation increased.\"\n\"Gas station 240m ahead.\"\n\"Customer says they\'re waiting outside.\"\n\"Achievement unlocked: Speedy Paws.\"\n\nAllow the player to open the phone while stopped and optionally while moving, but make this configurable for safety/gameplay reasons.\n\nCreate a complete **GPS navigation system**. The HUD should show a compact live map with the cat\'s position, current heading, pickup location, destination, route, nearby roads, gas stations, landmarks, and important points of interest. Include route recalculation if the player goes off-route. Add distance-to-destination, estimated arrival time, directional arrows, upcoming turns, and street names.\n\nCreate an expanded full-screen map. Allow players to zoom, pan, select destinations, view discovered locations, see available deliveries, locate gas stations, locate repair shops, and track exploration progress.\n\nThe GPS should have a playful cat personality. It can occasionally say things like \"Turn left, whiskers!\" or \"You\'re going the wrong way.\" Keep these moments rare so they remain funny.\n\nCreate a **navigation voice system** with optional voice prompts and sound cues.\n\nCreate a persistent **HUD** containing:\n\nMini-map\nFuel gauge\nCurrent speed\nCurrent coins\nCurrent rider level\nCurrent delivery objective\nDistance remaining\nETA\nVehicle condition\nDelivery timer when applicable\nPhone notification indicator\nWeather indicator\nCurrent street/district\n\nMake the HUD automatically minimize when appropriate to preserve immersion.\n\nCreate a full **touchscreen control system**:\n\nVirtual steering\nAccelerator\nBrake\nReverse\nHorn\nHandbrake\nCamera rotation\nLook-back button\nGPS/map button\nPhone button\nInteraction button\nDelivery confirmation\nVehicle lights\nOptional turn signals\n\nMake controls responsive, large, comfortable, and configurable. Allow the player to reposition or resize controls. Include multiple control schemes such as virtual joystick, steering wheel, button steering, and tilt controls if supported.\n\nImplement smooth third-person vehicle physics. Include acceleration, braking, turning, drifting, bumps, slopes, collisions, suspension movement, wheel rotation, skidding, and realistic-but-friendly vehicle handling. Prioritize fun and accessibility over strict realism.\n\nAdd traffic rules and road behavior. Include traffic lights, stop signs, crosswalks, pedestrian crossings, one-way streets, parking areas, and moving traffic. Do not make traffic unnecessarily punishing.\n\nCreate **minor collision consequences** rather than game-ending failures. A crash can reduce vehicle condition, cost a small repair fee, delay the delivery, or trigger a funny cat animation.\n\nAdd a **photo mode**. Let players stop anywhere, hide the HUD, rotate the camera, change camera distance, apply simple cinematic framing, and take screenshots of their cat and vehicle.\n\nCreate a **cat apartment/home base**. After completing deliveries, the cat can return home. The home should be customizable with furniture, cat beds, plants, posters, shelves, trophies, delivery memorabilia, and decorations. The cat can sleep, eat, relax, groom itself, and review delivery statistics.\n\nCreate a **home garage** where vehicles can be stored and customized.\n\nAdd a **pet/companion system** where the cat can eventually unlock a small companion animal that appears at home or accompanies certain missions. Keep it optional.\n\nCreate **mini-games** around the city. Examples:\n\nFuel-pump timing mini-game\nPackage sorting\nNewspaper folding\nQuick parking\nPackage stacking\nScooter cleaning\nCat grooming\nCoffee break\nDelivery bag organization\n\nKeep these activities optional.\n\nCreate **hidden secrets and collectibles** throughout the city. Add collectible fish, yarn balls, cat toys, newspaper clippings, postcards, paw prints, hidden alley murals, rooftop locations, and funny NPC encounters.\n\nCreate **side quests**. Examples:\n\nHelp a shopkeeper deliver something before closing.\nFind a lost package.\nDeliver flowers anonymously.\nDeliver a birthday cake.\nHelp a lost tourist find a location.\nDeliver newspapers to an elderly neighborhood.\nFind a customer\'s missing cat toy.\nDeliver an important fictional document.\nComplete a storm-time emergency supply route.\n\nCreate occasional **special events**:\n\nNight market\nFestival\nRainy-day rush\nHoliday deliveries\nFood festival\nStreet market\nNeighborhood fair\nCat convention\nCharity delivery day\nFireworks night\n\nDuring events, temporarily change the city, NPCs, traffic, music, delivery opportunities, and rewards.\n\nCreate a **weather-aware delivery economy** where certain jobs become more valuable during difficult conditions. For example, rain may create more food-delivery demand.\n\nCreate a **dynamic reputation system**. Reputation should rise from successful deliveries, good customer interactions, safe driving, exploration, and special missions. Higher reputation unlocks districts, customers, delivery categories, vehicles, cosmetics, and special jobs.\n\nCreate a **rider statistics dashboard** showing:\n\nTotal deliveries\nTotal distance traveled\nTotal coins earned\nTotal tips\nAverage delivery time\nBest delivery time\nHighest tip\nMost visited district\nFavorite vehicle\nFuel consumed\nNewspapers delivered\nCustomers served\nPerfect deliveries\nAccidents\nVehicles owned\nAchievements\n\nCreate **save/load persistence** so player progress, cat customization, money, vehicles, upgrades, achievements, map discoveries, home customization, and reputation persist.\n\nCreate a polished onboarding tutorial that teaches the player movement, steering, GPS, accepting jobs, pickup, delivery, fuel, gas stations, phone notifications, customer interaction, and rewards through an actual introductory delivery rather than a wall of instructions.\n\nCreate a **pause menu** with Resume, Map, Phone, Garage, Rider Profile, Settings, Controls, Audio, Graphics, Accessibility, Restart Delivery, and Save/Exit where supported.\n\nCreate accessibility options including adjustable UI scale, color-independent indicators, subtitle controls, reduced camera motion, reduced flashing effects, simplified controls, vibration toggle, audio sliders, navigation assistance, and automatic acceleration options.\n\nCreate rich sound design: cat meows, purring, engine sounds, brakes, tires, horns, traffic, rain, wind, birds, pedestrians, gas pumps, phone vibrations, notification sounds, UI taps, package pickup sounds, delivery completion sounds, reward sounds, achievement sounds, and contextual environmental audio.\n\nUse smooth animation everywhere. Buttons should animate when pressed. Maps should zoom smoothly. Menus should slide naturally. Notifications should appear elegantly. The cat should transition naturally between walking, mounting the scooter, riding, stopping, dismounting, picking up packages, handing packages to customers, celebrating, refueling, repairing the vehicle, and returning home.\n\nCreate a sophisticated camera system with:\n\nDriving camera\nClose driving camera\nNavigation camera\nFree-look camera\nRear-view camera\nCinematic delivery camera\nPhoto mode camera\n\nCamera movement should be smooth and physically convincing.\n\nCreate a complete fictional **Lalameow Rider city map** with districts that progressively unlock. Give every district a distinct visual identity and gameplay purpose.\n\nMake the game highly replayable through dynamically generated delivery jobs. Delivery requests should vary in location, distance, reward, package type, urgency, weather conditions, customer personality, and route complexity.\n\nAdd an **optional free-roam mode** where the player can explore the city without accepting a delivery.\n\nAdd a **delivery streak system** for consecutive successful deliveries, but make it forgiving. Streaks can generate small bonus rewards.\n\nAdd **combo bonuses** for efficient routes, multiple deliveries, perfect navigation, safe driving, and punctual completion.\n\nAdd **random world events**. While exploring, the cat may encounter:\n\nA road closure\nA street performance\nA traffic jam\nA broken-down vehicle\nA lost NPC\nA sudden rainstorm\nA market opening\nA parade\nA construction detour\nA friendly cat\nA stray animal\nA shortcut becoming available\nA surprise delivery opportunity\n\nThese should make the city feel unpredictable.\n\nAdd a **favorite locations system**. Players can bookmark locations on the map.\n\nAdd **fast travel** between unlocked districts, but balance it so free-roaming remains meaningful.\n\nAdd a **delivery history** screen showing recently completed jobs.\n\nAdd a **wallet** showing earnings, expenses, tips, fuel costs, repairs, and net income.\n\nAdd a fictional **Lalameow Rider Academy** where players can practice driving, braking, navigation, parking, fuel management, and advanced delivery routes.\n\nAdd driving tests that unlock advanced vehicles and districts.\n\nAdd a **training track** and obstacle course.\n\nCreate a **leaderboard-style personal records system**, but do not require online multiplayer. Players can compare their own best delivery times, longest routes, largest tips, and highest streaks.\n\nIf online functionality is supported later, structure the architecture so multiplayer or asynchronous features could eventually be added without rebuilding the entire game.\n\nCreate robust error handling and loading states. Never allow a missing asset, invalid delivery, unavailable route, or unexpected state to break the entire game. Use fallback behavior wherever appropriate.\n\nOptimize performance aggressively. Use efficient 3D models, level-of-detail systems, texture compression, object pooling, instancing, efficient lighting, optimized particles, streaming/loading between districts, and limited NPC simulation outside the player\'s immediate area.\n\nPrioritize the following playable vertical slice before adding secondary systems:\n\n1. Create and name the cat.\n2. Spawn the cat in its home.\n3. Enter the garage.\n4. Select a scooter.\n5. Enter the city.\n6. Receive a delivery notification.\n7. Open the phone.\n8. Inspect the delivery.\n9. Accept the delivery.\n10. See pickup and destination on the HUD map.\n11. Follow GPS navigation.\n12. Arrive at pickup.\n13. Pick up the package.\n14. See the package appear in the delivery bag.\n15. Drive to destination.\n16. Monitor fuel.\n17. Locate a gas station if necessary.\n18. Deliver the package.\n19. Interact with the customer.\n20. Receive payment and possible tip.\n21. Gain XP and reputation.\n22. Update delivery statistics.\n23. Receive another delivery.\n24. Continue playing seamlessly.\n\nOnly after this core loop is smooth and functional should the implementation expand into the larger city, advanced progression, home customization, side quests, events, collectibles, and other systems.\n\nThe ultimate design principle is simple: **make the player feel like they are living one small, charming life as a cat delivery rider.** The city should feel worth exploring even when there is no delivery. The cat should feel like a character rather than an avatar. The phone should feel useful. The GPS should feel alive. The vehicle should feel satisfying. Deliveries should create stories. Fuel, weather, customers, traffic, shortcuts, mistakes, tips, and unexpected encounters should naturally create little moments that players remember.\n\nThe final product should feel like a combination of a cozy life simulator, miniature open-world exploration game, arcade driving game, and delivery-management game, all wrapped around one central fantasy: **you are a cat, you have a scooter, your phone keeps ringing, and the whole city is waiting for you.**\n\n- Right now, the game is not yet playable. Can you make it a real game? I can barely recognize the game"
    }
  }
}
