    thinking: "**Analyzing Camera Parameters**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `updateCamera` function, specifically how it calculates camera distance and height based on player movement and the current camera mode. It seems the camera distance is dynamically adjusted with player speed, that\'s interesting. I\'m trying to see how these parameters are used in the overall camera positioning, especially the conversion to radians for the heading.\n\n\n**Investigating Camera Rotation**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `Math3D.kt` file. Specifically, I\'m examining how camera yaw affects the camera\'s view, which involves calculating `rotX` and `rotZ`. The current thought experiment involves the camera being behind the player relative to a heading angle. I\'ve formulated that the vector from the camera to the player is relative to the distance D, and heading angle alpha.\n\n\n**Validating Rotational Formulas**\n\nI\'ve been working through the mathematical formulas in `Math3D.kt`, testing the rotation calculations. I verified the yaw calculations that position the camera behind the player with a distance D and heading angle alpha. The pitch calculations were checked with example numbers. With the calculations complete, I am confident in their accuracy, now I will proceed to see how this translates into the projected screen coordinates for the camera view.\n\n\n**Addressing Angle Interpolation**\n\nI\'ve hit a crucial point regarding camera yaw. I realized the existing interpolation in `GameViewModel.kt` fails when the target yaw crosses the 0/360 degree boundary, resulting in incorrect rotation. I\'ve corrected the angular distance calculation with modular arithmetic, ensuring the camera rotates along the shortest path. I\'ve implemented a new shortest angle interpolation method.\n\n\n**Optimizing Redraw Logic**\n\nI\'ve just realized the `frameTimeNanos` variable is causing excessive recomposition in `GameScreen`. Updating `frameTimeNanos` within `LaunchedEffect` and reading it inside `Canvas` causes the entire screen to recompose 60 times a second! This impacts performance significantly. I need to rethink how to trigger redraws without recomposing the whole UI. This must be fixed!\n\n\n**Refining UI Redraw**\n\nI\'ve been exploring ways to optimize the screen redraw logic, moving away from the problematic `frameTimeNanos` approach. I am now looking at the `Modifier.drawWithContent { ... }` or `Modifier.drawBehind { ... }` functions. These seem promising because they allow me to trigger only the draw phase, which would greatly improve performance. I\'m considering using a dedicated `tick` or frame state in either `GameViewModel` or `GameScreen`, reading this tick only during the draw phase. I am going to verify this approach.\nI\'m also considering the character creator and onboarding feature from the user.\n\n\n**Developing Game Logic**\n\nOkay, I\'m working on the secondary systems now, starting with the onboarding. I\'ve broken down the steps, from cat creation and scooter selection to the delivery system. I\'m focusing on the first major interaction: a character creation screen for the cat rider! I\'ll implement options for cat name, breed, eye style, ears, and tail. This is exciting!\n\n\n"
