  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Scooter Drawing**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the 3D renderer, specifically `CatRiderRenderer3D.kt`. The scooter\'s side-view drawing, determined by lines 107-130, and the positioning of the wheels (`scale * 0.18f` offsets), really stands out. Now, I need to evaluate how a behind-the-vehicle perspective affects the overall presentation.\n\n\n**Visualizing Rear Perspective**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in envisioning the scooter from a rear perspective. I see the rear wheel centered, the license plate and taillight above it. The symmetrical footboard and fairing extend outwards. The delivery backpack and Lalameow logo are front and center. The cat\'s back and head, complete with helmet, are facing forward. Handlebars and mirrors protrude. The cat\'s tail sways, and of course, the lean of the scooter when turning! It\'s a significant shift from the old 2D side view.\n\n\n**Reimagining Gameplay Perspective**\n\nI\'ve realized the core issue: the renderer\'s perspective contradicts the intended rear-chase view, making the game unplayable. This warrants a complete redesign of `CatRiderRenderer3D`. I\'m now focusing on a true rear/isometric third-person 3D character renderer. Initially, I\'ll prioritize rear-chase view, responsive to camera yaw and scooter heading. I\'m starting to visualize the elements I\'ll need to define, such as the contact shadow, rear tire with tread and hub, mudguard/fender, license plate, and taillight.\n\n\n**Defining Scooter Features**\n\nI\'m now detailing the individual elements of the scooter. I\'m prioritizing the visual impact of the red brake light. I\'ve added a chrome exhaust pipe, angled right, emitting cartoon smoke when accelerating. The footboard and fairings now have chrome details, kickstand and pegs. The delivery backpack is high-capacity and prominent, with the Lalameow paw emblem, and reflective stripes. The cat\'s in a courier jacket, holding handlebars with its plush fur paws, tail swaying realistically.\n\n\n"
