  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Initiating Job Strategy**\n\nI\'m thinking, in the `init` section, we could kick things off with an active job right away, or at least offer one immediately. The idea is to begin with `_activeJob.value = createIntroDeliveryJob()`. This intro job could be something like \"Morning Express: Lily\'s Hot Croissants & Daily Purr Newspaper,\" with a `NEWSPAPER` or `PARCEL` category.\n\n\n**Defining Initial Delivery Task**\n\nI\'ve got the first job details mapped out: \"Lily\'s Artisan Bakery\" pickup, \"Grandma Whiskers\" dropoff with custom dialogue. This intro job will set `DeliveryState.ACCEPTED` with a waypoint to the bakery. I\'m visualizing the 3D parcel beacon pulsating! Then, after the pickup, it changes to `PICKED_UP`, loading the 3D model onto Nami\'s scooter and changing the beacon location. I\'m now transitioning to focus on the design for building rendering.\n\n\n**Calculating Visibility Checks**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the visibility calculations for the building rendering, specifically focusing on the face culling logic. I\'m projecting all eight vertices into camera space. I am working on checking each face\'s visibility based on the camera\'s position relative to its normal. I\'m using the dot product to determine which faces are pointing towards the camera. The goal is to optimize which faces need to be rendered for performance.\n\n\n**Optimizing Face Visibility**\n\nNow I\'m diving deeper into the face culling logic, and the calculations are surprisingly elegant. Using camera position in world space to check face visibility has yielded such simplicity and geometric accuracy! For each box, I\'m checking if the camera\'s Z position is less than the box\'s Z minus half its length to determine South face visibility, and so on. This approach eliminates inside-out walls with total accuracy. The math is beautifully clean!\n\n\n**Designing Storefront Rendering**\n\nMy thought process is now on the design of the street-facing building faces, and I\'m very excited about how they are turning out. I plan to draw faces according to their orientation to the street, with considerations for directional shading, glass windows, wooden doors with brass paw handles, and striped awnings. Each storefront will have a 3D sign with a shop name and emoji. I will add upper floor windows, roof cornices, and bold parapets. Also, I am now thinking about how WaypointRenderable can render 3D models. Specifically, there will be a glowing vertical beam with a pulsing effect.\n\n\n**Adding Road & Item Effects**\n\nI\'m now implementing animated rings for the asphalt road. Also, I\'m defining the 3D items, focusing on their visual details. A 3D cardboard parcel will be rotating and bobbing. I\'m also visualizing a rolled newspaper with a ribbon. Sparkle particles will orbit the floating items and there will be a billboard tag.\n\n\n**Visualizing Cat Rider Details**\n\nI am now focusing on the 3D model of the cat rider and scooter. I want to make sure the cat is visible and carries the delivery item correctly: a parcel with paw tape for parcels or newspaper bundles for newspapers. I\'m considering the cat\'s face (animated eyes, smiling mouth) and the scooter details (headlight, handlebars, chrome rim). I\'m also planning the front, angle, and rear views.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 125798063
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
