    thinking: "**Investigating RotX Usage**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a burning question now. I\'m focusing on line 79 in `Math3D.kt`, the one calculating `screenX`. Specifically, I\'m stuck on `rotX`. I need to figure out what `rotX` represents in this context. It seems like a pivotal variable, and its meaning is currently unclear to me. I need to understand how it fits into the calculation of the screen coordinate.\n\n\n**Deciphering Rotational Transformation**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! I\'ve traced `rotX`\'s origin to the `toCameraSpace` function, specifically the yaw rotation. It\'s calculated as `relX * cosY - relZ * sinY`. I\'m now understanding how the relative coordinates are transformed into camera space, using yaw and pitch. The yaw rotation seems to be around the Y-axis. Testing this by assuming the camera is at (0, 2.8, -6.5) facing in the direction of the +Z axis with an object at (5, 0, 0) makes the logic clearer.\n\n\n**Validating Coordinate Transformations**\n\nOkay, I\'m verifying the coordinate transformations! I\'m testing a +90 degree yaw, and I can now confirm an object in front of the camera should have a positive `rotZ` value. However, I\'m noticing an important sign discrepancy! A clockwise camera rotation means a counter-clockwise world rotation. This reveals that the rotation matrix uses the incorrect sign for `sin(yaw)`. I need to correct this. The correct transformation should use `cos(-yaw)` and `sin(-yaw)` for a clockwise rotation.\n\n\n**Addressing Rendering Issues**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the reasons behind the missing game world. First, the character creator was always open on launch because of an initialization issue. Second, the building rendering had critical bugs, including problematic near clipping and incomplete wall drawing. The near clipping issue involves incorrect z-clamping, causing projection problems for buildings behind the camera. I will address these issues by fixing near-plane polygon clipping.\n\n\n**Enhancing Game Aesthetics**\n\nOkay, I\'m now focused on significantly improving the game\'s visuals. I\'m thinking about adding several elements. I envision cute fabric-striped awnings over the sidewalks, and warm bakery interiors visible through shop windows. I\'m also planning to include 3D hanging shop signs and tactile sidewalks with curbs. I\'ve pinpointed an absolute necessity to implement 3D models for delivery parcels and newspapers, specifically cardboard boxes.\n\n\n**Visualizing Parcel Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into parcel enhancements! I\'m envisioning a craft paper texture parcel box with yellow Lalameow tape, a red fragile postage stamp, and a soft shadow. The parcel box or a rolled newspaper will strap onto the scooter\'s rear rack in 3D during deliveries. A floating pickup beacon will gently bob, highlighted by golden paw sparkles and a glowing ring! At the customer\'s location, a glowing delivery zone will appear. Upon delivery, the parcel will launch with a happy *POP*! I am thinking about adding visual enhancements for rolled newspapers and bakery croissant boxes next. I am also planning on updating the 3D playable character.\n\n\n**Designing Scooter Visuals**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the scooter\'s design, and imagining the visual updates! I\'m picturing a cinematic third-person chase mode from the get-go, with Nami zooming on her bright yellow Lalameow scooter. I\'m focusing on the yellow sculpted vintage body, the chrome headlight with a warm glow, and the rear-view mirrors. I envision rubber tires on silver rims, rotating with speed, and the insulated courier box with the Lalameow paw logo. I see a red LED taillight, puffing translucent smoke from the exhaust. I\'m also planning Nami\'s cute round fluffy cat head, twitching ears, and expressive eyes.\n\n\n"
