  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Renderable Classes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on defining the `BuildingRenderable` and `WaypointRenderable` classes. The `BuildingRenderable` takes a `CityBuilding` and a depth for 3D rendering. I plan to implement the `draw` method using `DrawScope`. Currently, my attention is on the specifics of this new class and related types and interfaces.\n\n\n**Refining 3D Rendering**\n\nI\'ve been working on refining the 3D rendering of `BuildingRenderable`. The recent focus has been on clipping and projecting points to the camera\'s view. I am using `camera.toCameraSpace` to calculate camera-space coordinates, and `camera.projectCameraPoint` to translate to screen coordinates, applying a z-clipping with `clampPt` to handle points behind the camera. I need to make sure buildings are not rendered if they are too close to the camera.\n\n\n**Implementing Face Visibility**\n\nI\'m now evaluating face visibility based on the camera\'s position relative to the building\'s planes. I calculate boolean flags, for each of the south, north, east, west, and roof faces. I\'m using these flags to make rendering decisions, for example, drawing the sidewalk plinth. Next I\'ll leverage a helper for drawing a wall, and then windows. I\'m also planning to incorporate a subtle contour edge.\n\nI\'m also determining conditional colors based on weather: neon night, golden sunset, and default conditions.\n\n\n**Developing Window Implementation**\n\nI\'m now in the thick of the window implementation within the wall rendering. My focus is on calculating window coordinates within the wall face. I calculate coordinates of the left and right window points, top and bottom. I\'m using path drawing for the window shape. The coordinates are using ratios, and `Offset` to create the path shape. The next step is to draw the path itself.\n\n\n**Finalizing Window Rendering**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the window implementation. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the final drawing steps. I\'ve calculated the window coordinates for two windows, and constructed paths using `moveTo`, `lineTo`, and `close`. I will draw the path using `drawScope.drawPath`, with a stroke style for the frames and then fill them with the window color.\n\n\n**Completing Wall Rendering**\n\nI\'ve finished the wall rendering, applying calculated shades based on face visibility. I\'m now drawing the south, north, west, and east walls conditionally, with different alpha values for a subtle shadowing effect. I\'m also drawing windows on those walls. Finally, I\'ve implemented the roof, including a parapet trim border. The next steps will consider storefront awnings and 3D signboards.\n\n\n**Adding Awning Implementation**\n\nI\'m now integrating storefront awnings on the building. I\'m focusing on the East face, and determining the `isFrontVisible` flag to render them. I\'m calculating the awning coordinates based on the building\'s geometry, using ratios of height and width. I\'m generating an awning path with a 3D stripe look. I\'m using `Offset` to construct the path and drawing it using `drawScope.drawPath` with a specified color.\n\n\n**Integrating Striped Awnings**\n\nI\'m expanding awning implementation with striped panels. I\'ve now added a loop to create a series of stripe panels on the awning. The calculations involve determining the start and end offsets for each stripe and constructing a new path that draws it. The panels use different points (left back, right back, right front, left front) to render the 3D stripe look. Each stripe panel is then drawn.\n\n\n**Refining Storefront Design**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the storefront. I\'ve been refining the awning implementation, focusing on the visual details. Specifically, I\'m defining a path for the storefront showcase window on the ground floor, working out its shape and position relative to the awning, which I will draw next. I\'m leveraging ratios and offsets to compute the window\'s corner coordinates, defining the window path with `moveTo`, `lineTo`, and `close`, ready for drawing.\n\n\n**Adding Storefront Door**\n\nI\'m now implementing the storefront door. I\'ve defined a path to create it on the ground floor. The design takes coordinates calculated earlier for the windows. I am using `moveTo`, `lineTo`, and `close` to construct the door. The path will be drawn next, filled with a brown color to simulate wood. Next, I\'ll integrate the storefront signboard.\n\n\n**Implementing Signboard Details**\n\nI\'m now incorporating a signboard on the storefront, and have the initial framework in place.  I\'m calculating the signboard\'s center position and determining a suitable scale based on depth and building dimensions.  Next, I\'ll draw the background with a pill-shaped rounded rectangle. After that, I\'m constructing a gold border around the sign. The next step is drawing the sign\'s text.\n\n\n**Visualizing Waypoint Emblems**\n\nI\'m now focusing on drawing the waypoint visuals. I\'m starting with concentric rings and a solid center circle, scaled according to depth. I\'m also implementing a \'golden paw\' emblem in the center, and these elements respond to the pulse variable.\n\n\n**Visualizing 3D Delivery Items**\n\nI\'m now rendering delivery items above the waypoints. I\'ve introduced a translucent 3D light column extending to the item. I\'m also implementing a floating item, which will bob up and down, and rotate based on the `pulse` variable. Currently, the display will vary depending on the delivery item\'s category. I\'m starting with a 3D rolled newspaper bundle.\n\n\n**Rendering Delivery Items**\n\nI\'m now rendering delivery items above the waypoint, specifically the 3D rolled newspaper bundle. I\'m using `drawRoundRect` to create the main cylindrical body of the paper roll with a white/cream color. Then, I\'m adding a side shadow effect using another rounded rectangle with a darker color. Finally, I\'m adding newspaper headline stripes with a line. Next, I\'ll be varying the package type.\n\n\n**Varying Item Presentation**\n\nI\'m now expanding the delivery item rendering beyond the newspaper. I\'m introducing a 3D cardboard parcel box, adapting existing calculations for its dimensions, and creating shadows for visual depth. The box is scaled based on the building size. I\'m adding a white paper texture on the front and top faces of the box to represent tape. Next, I\'ll refine the visual appeal.\n\n\n\n**Refining Package Presentation**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the visual details of the 3D cardboard parcel box. I\'ve introduced a bright yellow stripe to the paper texture on the front and top faces of the box to represent tape. Additionally, I\'m adding a stamped black cat paw print to the box, and have adjusted the top flap color. Next, I\'ll move on to adding variation to the package contents.\n\n\n\n**Adding Package Details**\n\nI\'m now incorporating additional details for the 3D cardboard parcel box, specifically a shipping label and a barcode. I\'m focusing on adding a \"postage label\" with a barcode to the front. The pawprint has been moved to one side. The next step is varying the visual presentation by delivery type.\n\n```\n```\n\n**Visualizing Parcel Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now implementing orbiting sparkle stars to enhance the visual presentation of the parcel delivery item. I\'m adding these around the central package. I\'m also constructing a floating destination marker badge. The next step is adding text to the badge.\n\n\\n\\n\nI\'m focusing on adding a floating destination marker badge, including calculations for its coordinates and size based on the item scale. I\'ve designed the badge, and created the background rectangle, and a differently colored inset. I will add text within the badge next.\n\n\n"
