    }
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "The core playable skeleton of **Lalameow Rider** is already working. Do NOT rebuild the existing gameplay architecture from scratch and do NOT replace functional systems unnecessarily. Treat the current project as the foundation of a real game that now needs a complete professional asset, UI/UX, animation, audio, environment, character, and visual-design pass. The goal is to transform the current basic prototype into a cohesive, adorable, polished, highly interactive 3D mobile game that feels intentionally designed from the ground up.\n\nFirst, inspect the entire existing codebase and identify what is already functional. Preserve working gameplay systems, player movement, delivery logic, GPS/navigation, fuel, map, phone, missions, progression, saving, and interaction systems wherever possible. Build the visual and experiential layer around the existing architecture rather than breaking it. If something is only a placeholder, replace it systematically with a production-quality asset or procedural equivalent. Avoid creating duplicate systems.\n\nEstablish a complete **Lalameow Rider Art Bible and Asset Library** inside the project. Every visual element should belong to one coherent art direction: adorable stylized 3D characters, miniature-city proportions, expressive faces, soft cinematic lighting, polished materials, colorful but sophisticated environments, rounded forms, subtle toy-like proportions, and premium game-quality presentation. The world should feel like a charming animated 3D universe rather than a collection of unrelated AI-generated assets.\n\nCreate a centralized asset registry so every asset has a logical category, unique identifier, reusable configuration, fallback asset, and clear relationship to the game system using it. Organize assets into:\n\nCHARACTERS\nPLAYER CAT\nNPCS\nCUSTOMERS\nDELIVERY RIDERS\nSHOPKEEPERS\nGAS-STATION WORKERS\nPEDESTRIANS\nANIMALS\nCOMPANIONS\nSPECIAL CHARACTERS\n\nVEHICLES\nSCOOTERS\nMOTORCYCLES\nELECTRIC SCOOTERS\nDELIVERY BIKES\nNPC VEHICLES\nBUSES\nTAXIS\nTRUCKS\nSERVICE VEHICLES\nSPECIAL VEHICLES\n\nENVIRONMENT\nBUILDINGS\nHOUSES\nAPARTMENTS\nRESTAURANTS\nCAF\303\211S\nBAKERIES\nCONVENIENCE STORES\nGROCERY STORES\nOFFICES\nWAREHOUSES\nGAS STATIONS\nREPAIR SHOPS\nPET SHOPS\nMARKETS\nPARKS\nSCHOOLS\nHOSPITAL-STYLE FICTIONAL LOCATIONS\nTRAIN STATIONS\nBUS STOPS\nBRIDGES\nTUNNELS\nROADS\nSIDEWALKS\nALLEYS\nROOFTOPS\nWATERFRONT\nMOUNTAIN AREAS\nLANDMARKS\n\nPROPS\nBENCHES\nTRASH CANS\nTRAFFIC LIGHTS\nSTREET SIGNS\nLAMPS\nHYDRANTS\nPARKING METERS\nMAILBOXES\nNEWSPAPER BOXES\nPLANTS\nTREES\nFLOWERS\nPOTS\nTABLES\nCHAIRS\nAWNINGS\nSTREET VENDORS\nDELIVERY BOXES\nPACKAGE CRATES\nCARD BOXES\nSHOP SIGNS\nDECORATIONS\n\nUI\nHUD\nMINI-MAP\nFULL MAP\nPHONE\nDELIVERY CARD\nBUTTONS\nICONS\nNOTIFICATIONS\nDIALOGUE\nMENUS\nGARAGE\nPROFILE\nSETTINGS\nSHOP\nINVENTORY\nACHIEVEMENTS\nMISSION SCREEN\nFUEL SYSTEM\nVEHICLE CONDITION\nLOADING SCREENS\nTUTORIAL\nPAUSE MENU\n\nAUDIO\nUI SOUNDS\nVEHICLE SOUNDS\nCAT SOUNDS\nNPC SOUNDS\nENVIRONMENT\nWEATHER\nDELIVERY SOUNDS\nPHONE SOUNDS\nMUSIC\nAMBIENCE\nSUCCESS SOUNDS\nFAILURE SOUNDS\nSPECIAL EVENT SOUNDS\n\nMOTION\nCHARACTER ANIMATIONS\nVEHICLE ANIMATIONS\nUI TRANSITIONS\nMAP ANIMATIONS\nPHONE ANIMATIONS\nDELIVERY ANIMATIONS\nENVIRONMENTAL ANIMATION\nCAMERA MOTION\nSPECIAL EFFECTS\n\nCreate a consistent **character design system**. The player cat must be a fully realized 3D character, not a primitive placeholder. Give the cat a recognizable silhouette, expressive eyes, animated ears, tail, paws, whiskers, mouth, helmet, jacket, delivery backpack, gloves, and footwear. Create idle, walking, running, mounting, dismounting, driving, braking, turning, looking around, checking phone, picking up packages, carrying packages, handing packages to customers, celebrating, falling, recovering, refueling, repairing, sleeping, eating, grooming, getting wet, shivering, laughing, becoming surprised, becoming tired, and victory animations.\n\nMake character animation modular so the same animation system can support different cat breeds and NPC characters.\n\nCreate a **character customization system** with interchangeable fur colors, patterns, eye colors, helmets, jackets, backpacks, accessories, collars, glasses, scarves, gloves, shoes, and other cosmetics. Make every cosmetic item compatible with the character rig.\n\nCreate a complete **NPC library**. NPCs should not all look like recolored versions of the same character. Create distinct silhouettes, body types, species, outfits, accessories, ages, professions, expressions, and personalities. Include cats, dogs, raccoons, foxes, rabbits, birds, bears, and other friendly fictional animals. Keep everything stylized and cohesive.\n\nCreate a **vehicle asset library** with multiple original scooters and motorcycles. Each vehicle needs a complete exterior model, wheels, tires, mirrors, headlights, brake lights, turn signals, seat, handlebars, delivery storage, dashboard, exhaust or electric components, and appropriate animation points. Add subtle suspension movement and wheel rotation. Vehicle cosmetics must be modular.\n\nCreate a complete **building-generation system**. Buildings should not be simple colored cubes. Build modular fa\303\247ades with windows, doors, balconies, roofs, signs, awnings, lights, air-conditioning units, plants, advertisements, street numbers, delivery entrances, storefronts, and architectural details. Create multiple building families so neighborhoods have visual variety.\n\nCreate procedural variations for buildings using modular components. The same underlying building system should be capable of generating many visually different buildings without requiring hundreds of manually modeled assets.\n\nCreate distinct visual identities for every city district. For example:\n\nCENTRAL CITY \342\200\224 dense, colorful, modern, busy streets and tall buildings.\n\nOLD TOWN \342\200\224 narrow streets, warm architecture, small shops, balconies, older fa\303\247ades.\n\nMARKET DISTRICT \342\200\224 stalls, awnings, crates, food vendors, colorful signs.\n\nWATERFRONT \342\200\224 promenade, boats, bridges, caf\303\251s, reflections, open sky.\n\nINDUSTRIAL DISTRICT \342\200\224 warehouses, loading bays, trucks, utility structures.\n\nRESIDENTIAL DISTRICT \342\200\224 houses, gardens, apartment buildings, quiet streets.\n\nNIGHT DISTRICT \342\200\224 neon-inspired fictional signage, restaurants, nightlife, glowing windows.\n\nMOUNTAIN DISTRICT \342\200\224 slopes, winding roads, trees, scenic viewpoints.\n\nDo not make these districts look like simple recolors. Give them genuinely different architectural language, props, lighting, road layouts, vegetation, NPC activity, and ambience.\n\nCreate a complete **road and street asset kit**. Include straight roads, curves, intersections, roundabouts, parking areas, sidewalks, crosswalks, traffic lights, lane markings, road signs, speed signs, barriers, construction zones, bridges, ramps, tunnels, alleys, shortcuts, and pedestrian areas. Everything should snap together cleanly.\n\nCreate a **lighting system** with beautiful daytime, sunset, nighttime, rainy, cloudy, foggy, and dawn lighting. Buildings should have interior window lights at night. Streetlights should turn on automatically. Storefronts should glow. Wet roads should have subtle reflections during rain.\n\nCreate a complete **weather visual-effects library**. Add rain particles, puddles, wet surfaces, droplets on the camera, fog, clouds, wind-blown leaves, splashes, lightning-style atmospheric effects if appropriate, and weather-specific environmental reactions. Keep effects performant on mobile.\n\nCreate a complete **VFX library** for gameplay:\n\nDelivery completed\nCoins earned\nTip received\nXP gained\nLevel up\nAchievement unlocked\nFuel warning\nLow battery/vehicle warning if applicable\nGPS route recalculation\nWrong-way warning\nPackage pickup\nPackage delivery\nCustomer rating\nVehicle collision\nBrake effect\nTire skid\nRain splash\nWater puddle\nPhone notification\nNew mission\nSpecial event\nConfetti\nCat celebration\nSpeed boost\nFuel refill\n\nEvery VFX should have a consistent art style.\n\nBuild a professional **UI/UX design system**. Do not use generic browser controls. Replace placeholder buttons with custom game UI.\n\nCreate reusable components for:\n\nPrimary button\nSecondary button\nIcon button\nToggle\nSlider\nProgress bar\nXP bar\nFuel gauge\nVehicle condition gauge\nMap marker\nGPS arrow\nMission card\nDelivery card\nNotification card\nDialogue bubble\nReward popup\nCoin counter\nLevel indicator\nTimer\nDistance indicator\nPhone notification\nInventory slot\nVehicle card\nCharacter card\nCustomization item\nAchievement badge\nTab navigation\nModal window\nTooltip\nLoading indicator\nConfirmation dialog\n\nEvery component needs hover/press/active/disabled/loading states where relevant, smooth transitions, tactile feedback, and mobile-friendly hit areas.\n\nDesign the HUD specifically for touchscreen gameplay. It should communicate information quickly without covering the game world. Make the mini-map attractive and readable. Add animated GPS route lines, destination markers, pickup markers, gas-station markers, player direction, and nearby points of interest.\n\nCreate a **full-screen map experience** with animated zooming and panning, district boundaries, discovered locations, delivery locations, gas stations, repair shops, landmarks, shortcuts, and player position. Use an original fictional map style rather than copying real-world navigation applications.\n\nCreate the **Lalameow smartphone** as a polished interactive object. Give it a beautiful phone frame, animated opening/closing, notification animations, vibration-style visual feedback, app icons, message bubbles, delivery requests, GPS, wallet, rider profile, weather, achievements, settings, and delivery history.\n\nCreate a complete **delivery request interface**. When a new job arrives, the card should animate onto the phone/HUD with:\n\nPickup\nDestination\nPackage type\nDistance\nEstimated time\nReward\nTip possibility\nFuel estimate\nUrgency\nDifficulty\nSpecial instructions\n\nGive ACCEPT and DECLINE buttons strong visual hierarchy.\n\nCreate a **delivery interaction sequence**. At pickup, show the package being physically collected and placed into the delivery bag. At the destination, show the cat dismounting, carrying the package, approaching the customer, handing it over, and receiving payment. If full 3D animation is technically too expensive, use a carefully designed hybrid animation rather than simply changing a number on screen.\n\nCreate a **fuel system visual language**. The fuel gauge should be beautiful and immediately understandable. When fuel is low, animate the gauge subtly and send a phone notification. When refueling, create a satisfying filling animation, fuel sound, payment interaction, and completion effect.\n\nCreate a complete **gas station asset kit** including pumps, canopy, signs, shop building, parking spaces, price displays using fictional in-game currency, trash cans, lights, vehicles, staff NPCs, and environmental props. Make gas stations visually recognizable from a distance.\n\nCreate a complete **repair-shop asset kit** with garage doors, lifts, tools, tires, mechanics, spare parts, signage, and vehicle service animations.\n\nCreate a **home/apartment interior** for the player. Include a bed, cat furniture, desk, phone charger, wardrobe, trophy shelf, garage access, delivery memorabilia, plants, windows, decorations, and customizable furniture. Add animations for sleeping, eating, relaxing, grooming, and checking statistics.\n\nCreate a **garage interface** where the player can see their vehicles in 3D, rotate them, change cosmetics, upgrade statistics, inspect condition, and select a vehicle.\n\nCreate a **cosmetic shop** with rotating 3D previews. Players should be able to preview helmets, jackets, backpacks, accessories, vehicle decals, wheels, lights, and other items before purchasing them with in-game currency.\n\nCreate a complete **sound-effects library**. Every interaction should have appropriate audio. Avoid generic placeholder beeps.\n\nInclude:\n\nSoft UI taps\nButton clicks\nMap clicks\nPhone vibration\nPhone notification\nMessage received\nGPS turn prompt\nGPS recalculation\nDelivery accepted\nDelivery declined\nPackage pickup\nPackage placed in bag\nPackage delivered\nCustomer greeting\nCustomer reaction\nTip received\nCoins earned\nXP earned\nLevel up\nAchievement\nFuel pump\nFuel flowing\nPayment\nVehicle ignition\nEngine idle\nAcceleration\nBraking\nTire skid\nHorn\nTurn signal\nCrash\nRepair\nCat meows\nCat purrs\nCat surprised sound\nCat happy sound\nCat tired sound\nCat angry sound\nCat sneeze\nRain\nThunder\nWind\nBirds\nTraffic\nPedestrians\nMarket ambience\nWaterfront ambience\nNight ambience\n\nCreate an adaptive **music system**. Use different musical moods for daytime exploration, delivery missions, nighttime, rain, special events, home, garage, map, menu, and achievement screens. Music should transition smoothly rather than abruptly restarting.\n\nCreate an **ambient soundscape system** so each district sounds different.\n\nCreate a complete **UI motion system**. Nothing should simply appear or disappear unless necessary. Use short, polished animations for menus, cards, buttons, notifications, maps, rewards, level-ups, phone screens, delivery completion, and transitions. Motion should communicate hierarchy and state rather than exist merely as decoration.\n\nCreate a complete **camera-motion system**. Use subtle camera shake for acceleration, braking, collisions, jumps, landing, rain, and cinematic moments. Add smooth camera transitions between driving, walking, interaction, map, phone, garage, and delivery scenes. Include an accessibility option to reduce camera motion.\n\nCreate a **gameplay feedback system**. Every important action should provide at least two forms of feedback where appropriate: visual, audio, animation, haptic-style UI feedback, or camera response.\n\nCreate a **loading and transition system** with beautiful Lalameow-themed loading screens. Show rotating tips, cat illustrations, delivery facts, fictional city facts, and gameplay hints. Avoid blank loading screens.\n\nCreate **empty states and error states** that are also designed. For example:\n\nNo deliveries available\nNo fuel\nNo map route\nNo notifications\nNo achievements\nNo vehicles\nNo inventory\nConnection unavailable\nSave failure\nAsset loading\nDistrict locked\n\nNever expose raw technical errors to the player.\n\nCreate a **tutorial presentation system** using animated callouts, arrows, paw indicators, highlighted buttons, and short explanations. Never overwhelm the player with paragraphs.\n\nCreate **asset fallback logic**. If a sophisticated 3D asset cannot be loaded, use a visually appropriate fallback rather than an ugly primitive. The game should remain visually coherent even under limited hardware.\n\nCreate a centralized **theme/token system** for colors, typography, spacing, corner radii, shadows, iconography, animation duration, easing curves, particle density, and UI scale. Every interface should use these shared tokens.\n\nUse a consistent visual hierarchy: cheerful and colorful, but not childish or visually chaotic. The game should look premium enough to be presented as a real commercial game.\n\nCreate an **asset preview/development gallery** accessible through a development-only menu. It should allow inspection of characters, vehicles, buildings, props, UI components, icons, animations, VFX, audio categories, and environments. This will make it easy to verify that the entire asset library is actually connected to the game.\n\nCreate an **asset usage audit** so unused assets can be identified and duplicate assets avoided.\n\nCreate an **asset loading manager** with intelligent caching and lazy loading. Do not load the entire city and every asset simultaneously on mobile. Load assets based on the player\'s current district and proximity.\n\nCreate level-of-detail strategies for characters, buildings, vehicles, props, and effects. Reduce complexity when objects are far away. Prioritize stable frame rates and smooth controls.\n\nCreate a **quality-settings system** with Low, Medium, High, and Ultra where technically appropriate. Allow adjustments to shadows, reflections, particles, draw distance, NPC density, texture quality, effects, and environmental detail.\n\nMake the game feel polished at every level. A player should be able to look at a tiny object such as a gas pump, mailbox, newspaper box, traffic light, delivery bag, or phone icon and feel that it belongs to the same universe.\n\nMost importantly, do not merely generate a large pile of disconnected assets. **Integrate the assets into the existing game.** The player cat must actually use the animations. Vehicles must use their models. Buildings must populate the city. UI components must replace placeholder controls. Sounds must be triggered by gameplay events. VFX must respond to gameplay. NPCs must use the character library. Weather must use the VFX library. Gas stations must interact with the fuel system. Repair shops must interact with vehicle condition. The phone must interact with deliveries. The map must interact with navigation. The garage must interact with vehicles and customization.\n\nAfter implementation, perform a complete visual and interaction audit of every major screen and gameplay state. Look for placeholder cubes, default browser buttons, missing icons, empty areas, inconsistent fonts, inconsistent spacing, unanimated controls, silent interactions, broken transitions, duplicated assets, generic UI, and unfinished visual states. Replace or improve them.\n\nDo not stop after generating the asset library. Connect the library to the playable experience.\n\nThe desired result is:\n\n**Lalameow Rider \342\200\224 a living miniature city where a customizable cat has a scooter, a phone that keeps ringing, deliveries to make, fuel to manage, customers to meet, places to explore, and a world full of tiny surprises.**\n\nThe current game already provides the skeleton. Your job now is to give that skeleton its **face, fur, clothing, personality, city, buildings, sounds, motion, atmosphere, interface, and soul.**"
    items {
      text: "The core playable skeleton of **Lalameow Rider** is already working. Do NOT rebuild the existing gameplay architecture from scratch and do NOT replace functional systems unnecessarily. Treat the current project as the foundation of a real game that now needs a complete professional asset, UI/UX, animation, audio, environment, character, and visual-design pass. The goal is to transform the current basic prototype into a cohesive, adorable, polished, highly interactive 3D mobile game that feels intentionally designed from the ground up.\n\nFirst, inspect the entire existing codebase and identify what is already functional. Preserve working gameplay systems, player movement, delivery logic, GPS/navigation, fuel, map, phone, missions, progression, saving, and interaction systems wherever possible. Build the visual and experiential layer around the existing architecture rather than breaking it. If something is only a placeholder, replace it systematically with a production-quality asset or procedural equivalent. Avoid creating duplicate systems.\n\nEstablish a complete **Lalameow Rider Art Bible and Asset Library** inside the project. Every visual element should belong to one coherent art direction: adorable stylized 3D characters, miniature-city proportions, expressive faces, soft cinematic lighting, polished materials, colorful but sophisticated environments, rounded forms, subtle toy-like proportions, and premium game-quality presentation. The world should feel like a charming animated 3D universe rather than a collection of unrelated AI-generated assets.\n\nCreate a centralized asset registry so every asset has a logical category, unique identifier, reusable configuration, fallback asset, and clear relationship to the game system using it. Organize assets into:\n\nCHARACTERS\nPLAYER CAT\nNPCS\nCUSTOMERS\nDELIVERY RIDERS\nSHOPKEEPERS\nGAS-STATION WORKERS\nPEDESTRIANS\nANIMALS\nCOMPANIONS\nSPECIAL CHARACTERS\n\nVEHICLES\nSCOOTERS\nMOTORCYCLES\nELECTRIC SCOOTERS\nDELIVERY BIKES\nNPC VEHICLES\nBUSES\nTAXIS\nTRUCKS\nSERVICE VEHICLES\nSPECIAL VEHICLES\n\nENVIRONMENT\nBUILDINGS\nHOUSES\nAPARTMENTS\nRESTAURANTS\nCAF\303\211S\nBAKERIES\nCONVENIENCE STORES\nGROCERY STORES\nOFFICES\nWAREHOUSES\nGAS STATIONS\nREPAIR SHOPS\nPET SHOPS\nMARKETS\nPARKS\nSCHOOLS\nHOSPITAL-STYLE FICTIONAL LOCATIONS\nTRAIN STATIONS\nBUS STOPS\nBRIDGES\nTUNNELS\nROADS\nSIDEWALKS\nALLEYS\nROOFTOPS\nWATERFRONT\nMOUNTAIN AREAS\nLANDMARKS\n\nPROPS\nBENCHES\nTRASH CANS\nTRAFFIC LIGHTS\nSTREET SIGNS\nLAMPS\nHYDRANTS\nPARKING METERS\nMAILBOXES\nNEWSPAPER BOXES\nPLANTS\nTREES\nFLOWERS\nPOTS\nTABLES\nCHAIRS\nAWNINGS\nSTREET VENDORS\nDELIVERY BOXES\nPACKAGE CRATES\nCARD BOXES\nSHOP SIGNS\nDECORATIONS\n\nUI\nHUD\nMINI-MAP\nFULL MAP\nPHONE\nDELIVERY CARD\nBUTTONS\nICONS\nNOTIFICATIONS\nDIALOGUE\nMENUS\nGARAGE\nPROFILE\nSETTINGS\nSHOP\nINVENTORY\nACHIEVEMENTS\nMISSION SCREEN\nFUEL SYSTEM\nVEHICLE CONDITION\nLOADING SCREENS\nTUTORIAL\nPAUSE MENU\n\nAUDIO\nUI SOUNDS\nVEHICLE SOUNDS\nCAT SOUNDS\nNPC SOUNDS\nENVIRONMENT\nWEATHER\nDELIVERY SOUNDS\nPHONE SOUNDS\nMUSIC\nAMBIENCE\nSUCCESS SOUNDS\nFAILURE SOUNDS\nSPECIAL EVENT SOUNDS\n\nMOTION\nCHARACTER ANIMATIONS\nVEHICLE ANIMATIONS\nUI TRANSITIONS\nMAP ANIMATIONS\nPHONE ANIMATIONS\nDELIVERY ANIMATIONS\nENVIRONMENTAL ANIMATION\nCAMERA MOTION\nSPECIAL EFFECTS\n\nCreate a consistent **character design system**. The player cat must be a fully realized 3D character, not a primitive placeholder. Give the cat a recognizable silhouette, expressive eyes, animated ears, tail, paws, whiskers, mouth, helmet, jacket, delivery backpack, gloves, and footwear. Create idle, walking, running, mounting, dismounting, driving, braking, turning, looking around, checking phone, picking up packages, carrying packages, handing packages to customers, celebrating, falling, recovering, refueling, repairing, sleeping, eating, grooming, getting wet, shivering, laughing, becoming surprised, becoming tired, and victory animations.\n\nMake character animation modular so the same animation system can support different cat breeds and NPC characters.\n\nCreate a **character customization system** with interchangeable fur colors, patterns, eye colors, helmets, jackets, backpacks, accessories, collars, glasses, scarves, gloves, shoes, and other cosmetics. Make every cosmetic item compatible with the character rig.\n\nCreate a complete **NPC library**. NPCs should not all look like recolored versions of the same character. Create distinct silhouettes, body types, species, outfits, accessories, ages, professions, expressions, and personalities. Include cats, dogs, raccoons, foxes, rabbits, birds, bears, and other friendly fictional animals. Keep everything stylized and cohesive.\n\nCreate a **vehicle asset library** with multiple original scooters and motorcycles. Each vehicle needs a complete exterior model, wheels, tires, mirrors, headlights, brake lights, turn signals, seat, handlebars, delivery storage, dashboard, exhaust or electric components, and appropriate animation points. Add subtle suspension movement and wheel rotation. Vehicle cosmetics must be modular.\n\nCreate a complete **building-generation system**. Buildings should not be simple colored cubes. Build modular fa\303\247ades with windows, doors, balconies, roofs, signs, awnings, lights, air-conditioning units, plants, advertisements, street numbers, delivery entrances, storefronts, and architectural details. Create multiple building families so neighborhoods have visual variety.\n\nCreate procedural variations for buildings using modular components. The same underlying building system should be capable of generating many visually different buildings without requiring hundreds of manually modeled assets.\n\nCreate distinct visual identities for every city district. For example:\n\nCENTRAL CITY \342\200\224 dense, colorful, modern, busy streets and tall buildings.\n\nOLD TOWN \342\200\224 narrow streets, warm architecture, small shops, balconies, older fa\303\247ades.\n\nMARKET DISTRICT \342\200\224 stalls, awnings, crates, food vendors, colorful signs.\n\nWATERFRONT \342\200\224 promenade, boats, bridges, caf\303\251s, reflections, open sky.\n\nINDUSTRIAL DISTRICT \342\200\224 warehouses, loading bays, trucks, utility structures.\n\nRESIDENTIAL DISTRICT \342\200\224 houses, gardens, apartment buildings, quiet streets.\n\nNIGHT DISTRICT \342\200\224 neon-inspired fictional signage, restaurants, nightlife, glowing windows.\n\nMOUNTAIN DISTRICT \342\200\224 slopes, winding roads, trees, scenic viewpoints.\n\nDo not make these districts look like simple recolors. Give them genuinely different architectural language, props, lighting, road layouts, vegetation, NPC activity, and ambience.\n\nCreate a complete **road and street asset kit**. Include straight roads, curves, intersections, roundabouts, parking areas, sidewalks, crosswalks, traffic lights, lane markings, road signs, speed signs, barriers, construction zones, bridges, ramps, tunnels, alleys, shortcuts, and pedestrian areas. Everything should snap together cleanly.\n\nCreate a **lighting system** with beautiful daytime, sunset, nighttime, rainy, cloudy, foggy, and dawn lighting. Buildings should have interior window lights at night. Streetlights should turn on automatically. Storefronts should glow. Wet roads should have subtle reflections during rain.\n\nCreate a complete **weather visual-effects library**. Add rain particles, puddles, wet surfaces, droplets on the camera, fog, clouds, wind-blown leaves, splashes, lightning-style atmospheric effects if appropriate, and weather-specific environmental reactions. Keep effects performant on mobile.\n\nCreate a complete **VFX library** for gameplay:\n\nDelivery completed\nCoins earned\nTip received\nXP gained\nLevel up\nAchievement unlocked\nFuel warning\nLow battery/vehicle warning if applicable\nGPS route recalculation\nWrong-way warning\nPackage pickup\nPackage delivery\nCustomer rating\nVehicle collision\nBrake effect\nTire skid\nRain splash\nWater puddle\nPhone notification\nNew mission\nSpecial event\nConfetti\nCat celebration\nSpeed boost\nFuel refill\n\nEvery VFX should have a consistent art style.\n\nBuild a professional **UI/UX design system**. Do not use generic browser controls. Replace placeholder buttons with custom game UI.\n\nCreate reusable components for:\n\nPrimary button\nSecondary button\nIcon button\nToggle\nSlider\nProgress bar\nXP bar\nFuel gauge\nVehicle condition gauge\nMap marker\nGPS arrow\nMission card\nDelivery card\nNotification card\nDialogue bubble\nReward popup\nCoin counter\nLevel indicator\nTimer\nDistance indicator\nPhone notification\nInventory slot\nVehicle card\nCharacter card\nCustomization item\nAchievement badge\nTab navigation\nModal window\nTooltip\nLoading indicator\nConfirmation dialog\n\nEvery component needs hover/press/active/disabled/loading states where relevant, smooth transitions, tactile feedback, and mobile-friendly hit areas.\n\nDesign the HUD specifically for touchscreen gameplay. It should communicate information quickly without covering the game world. Make the mini-map attractive and readable. Add animated GPS route lines, destination markers, pickup markers, gas-station markers, player direction, and nearby points of interest.\n\nCreate a **full-screen map experience** with animated zooming and panning, district boundaries, discovered locations, delivery locations, gas stations, repair shops, landmarks, shortcuts, and player position. Use an original fictional map style rather than copying real-world navigation applications.\n\nCreate the **Lalameow smartphone** as a polished interactive object. Give it a beautiful phone frame, animated opening/closing, notification animations, vibration-style visual feedback, app icons, message bubbles, delivery requests, GPS, wallet, rider profile, weather, achievements, settings, and delivery history.\n\nCreate a complete **delivery request interface**. When a new job arrives, the card should animate onto the phone/HUD with:\n\nPickup\nDestination\nPackage type\nDistance\nEstimated time\nReward\nTip possibility\nFuel estimate\nUrgency\nDifficulty\nSpecial instructions\n\nGive ACCEPT and DECLINE buttons strong visual hierarchy.\n\nCreate a **delivery interaction sequence**. At pickup, show the package being physically collected and placed into the delivery bag. At the destination, show the cat dismounting, carrying the package, approaching the customer, handing it over, and receiving payment. If full 3D animation is technically too expensive, use a carefully designed hybrid animation rather than simply changing a number on screen.\n\nCreate a **fuel system visual language**. The fuel gauge should be beautiful and immediately understandable. When fuel is low, animate the gauge subtly and send a phone notification. When refueling, create a satisfying filling animation, fuel sound, payment interaction, and completion effect.\n\nCreate a complete **gas station asset kit** including pumps, canopy, signs, shop building, parking spaces, price displays using fictional in-game currency, trash cans, lights, vehicles, staff NPCs, and environmental props. Make gas stations visually recognizable from a distance.\n\nCreate a complete **repair-shop asset kit** with garage doors, lifts, tools, tires, mechanics, spare parts, signage, and vehicle service animations.\n\nCreate a **home/apartment interior** for the player. Include a bed, cat furniture, desk, phone charger, wardrobe, trophy shelf, garage access, delivery memorabilia, plants, windows, decorations, and customizable furniture. Add animations for sleeping, eating, relaxing, grooming, and checking statistics.\n\nCreate a **garage interface** where the player can see their vehicles in 3D, rotate them, change cosmetics, upgrade statistics, inspect condition, and select a vehicle.\n\nCreate a **cosmetic shop** with rotating 3D previews. Players should be able to preview helmets, jackets, backpacks, accessories, vehicle decals, wheels, lights, and other items before purchasing them with in-game currency.\n\nCreate a complete **sound-effects library**. Every interaction should have appropriate audio. Avoid generic placeholder beeps.\n\nInclude:\n\nSoft UI taps\nButton clicks\nMap clicks\nPhone vibration\nPhone notification\nMessage received\nGPS turn prompt\nGPS recalculation\nDelivery accepted\nDelivery declined\nPackage pickup\nPackage placed in bag\nPackage delivered\nCustomer greeting\nCustomer reaction\nTip received\nCoins earned\nXP earned\nLevel up\nAchievement\nFuel pump\nFuel flowing\nPayment\nVehicle ignition\nEngine idle\nAcceleration\nBraking\nTire skid\nHorn\nTurn signal\nCrash\nRepair\nCat meows\nCat purrs\nCat surprised sound\nCat happy sound\nCat tired sound\nCat angry sound\nCat sneeze\nRain\nThunder\nWind\nBirds\nTraffic\nPedestrians\nMarket ambience\nWaterfront ambience\nNight ambience\n\nCreate an adaptive **music system**. Use different musical moods for daytime exploration, delivery missions, nighttime, rain, special events, home, garage, map, menu, and achievement screens. Music should transition smoothly rather than abruptly restarting.\n\nCreate an **ambient soundscape system** so each district sounds different.\n\nCreate a complete **UI motion system**. Nothing should simply appear or disappear unless necessary. Use short, polished animations for menus, cards, buttons, notifications, maps, rewards, level-ups, phone screens, delivery completion, and transitions. Motion should communicate hierarchy and state rather than exist merely as decoration.\n\nCreate a complete **camera-motion system**. Use subtle camera shake for acceleration, braking, collisions, jumps, landing, rain, and cinematic moments. Add smooth camera transitions between driving, walking, interaction, map, phone, garage, and delivery scenes. Include an accessibility option to reduce camera motion.\n\nCreate a **gameplay feedback system**. Every important action should provide at least two forms of feedback where appropriate: visual, audio, animation, haptic-style UI feedback, or camera response.\n\nCreate a **loading and transition system** with beautiful Lalameow-themed loading screens. Show rotating tips, cat illustrations, delivery facts, fictional city facts, and gameplay hints. Avoid blank loading screens.\n\nCreate **empty states and error states** that are also designed. For example:\n\nNo deliveries available\nNo fuel\nNo map route\nNo notifications\nNo achievements\nNo vehicles\nNo inventory\nConnection unavailable\nSave failure\nAsset loading\nDistrict locked\n\nNever expose raw technical errors to the player.\n\nCreate a **tutorial presentation system** using animated callouts, arrows, paw indicators, highlighted buttons, and short explanations. Never overwhelm the player with paragraphs.\n\nCreate **asset fallback logic**. If a sophisticated 3D asset cannot be loaded, use a visually appropriate fallback rather than an ugly primitive. The game should remain visually coherent even under limited hardware.\n\nCreate a centralized **theme/token system** for colors, typography, spacing, corner radii, shadows, iconography, animation duration, easing curves, particle density, and UI scale. Every interface should use these shared tokens.\n\nUse a consistent visual hierarchy: cheerful and colorful, but not childish or visually chaotic. The game should look premium enough to be presented as a real commercial game.\n\nCreate an **asset preview/development gallery** accessible through a development-only menu. It should allow inspection of characters, vehicles, buildings, props, UI components, icons, animations, VFX, audio categories, and environments. This will make it easy to verify that the entire asset library is actually connected to the game.\n\nCreate an **asset usage audit** so unused assets can be identified and duplicate assets avoided.\n\nCreate an **asset loading manager** with intelligent caching and lazy loading. Do not load the entire city and every asset simultaneously on mobile. Load assets based on the player\'s current district and proximity.\n\nCreate level-of-detail strategies for characters, buildings, vehicles, props, and effects. Reduce complexity when objects are far away. Prioritize stable frame rates and smooth controls.\n\nCreate a **quality-settings system** with Low, Medium, High, and Ultra where technically appropriate. Allow adjustments to shadows, reflections, particles, draw distance, NPC density, texture quality, effects, and environmental detail.\n\nMake the game feel polished at every level. A player should be able to look at a tiny object such as a gas pump, mailbox, newspaper box, traffic light, delivery bag, or phone icon and feel that it belongs to the same universe.\n\nMost importantly, do not merely generate a large pile of disconnected assets. **Integrate the assets into the existing game.** The player cat must actually use the animations. Vehicles must use their models. Buildings must populate the city. UI components must replace placeholder controls. Sounds must be triggered by gameplay events. VFX must respond to gameplay. NPCs must use the character library. Weather must use the VFX library. Gas stations must interact with the fuel system. Repair shops must interact with vehicle condition. The phone must interact with deliveries. The map must interact with navigation. The garage must interact with vehicles and customization.\n\nAfter implementation, perform a complete visual and interaction audit of every major screen and gameplay state. Look for placeholder cubes, default browser buttons, missing icons, empty areas, inconsistent fonts, inconsistent spacing, unanimated controls, silent interactions, broken transitions, duplicated assets, generic UI, and unfinished visual states. Replace or improve them.\n\nDo not stop after generating the asset library. Connect the library to the playable experience.\n\nThe desired result is:\n\n**Lalameow Rider \342\200\224 a living miniature city where a customizable cat has a scooter, a phone that keeps ringing, deliveries to make, fuel to manage, customers to meet, places to explore, and a world full of tiny surprises.**\n\nThe current game already provides the skeleton. Your job now is to give that skeleton its **face, fur, clothing, personality, city, buildings, sounds, motion, atmosphere, interface, and soul.**"
    }
  }
}
